course: dictionary
lesson: 7576
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word pressure is a good example. «Vent water can be hotter than three hundred degrees, but it does not boil because of the huge pressure of the deep sea.» Here pressure is the heavy push of water on everything down there.
explain: «In a group, the wish to fit in makes risky things feel normal; this is peer pressure.» Now pressure means the feeling of being pushed by friends to do something. The clues are the group and fit in. Ask: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Pressure can also be a verb. To pressure someone means to try hard to make them do something. You can say no to peer pressure, so nobody has to do what they do not want to do.
fact: Pressure means how hard something pushes on a surface.
fact: Air pressure and water pressure are the pushing force of air or water.
fact: «Peer pressure» is when friends push you to do things you don't want to do.
fact: On a smaller area, the same push makes more pressure, so a sharp knife cuts.
fact: Phrases with pressure include under pressure, blood pressure, tyre pressure and apply pressure.
exercise: «When you breathe in, the pressure inside drops, and outside air pushes in.» What does pressure mean here? | Something you press with your hand | A feeling of being rushed | *The pushing force of the air inside your body | A kind of muscle
exercise: «In a group, the wish to fit in makes risky things feel normal; this is peer pressure.» What is peer pressure? | *Friends pushing you to do things you don't want to do | Air pushing into your lungs | A test you must do well in | The weight of a heavy bag
exercise: «The snailfish has soft bones, so the pressure does not crush it.» What does pressure mean here? | Friendly feelings between fish | *The huge push of water on everything deep down | A stone that is heavy to lift | The feeling of being pushed to do well
test: Which meaning fits in «She felt pressure to win the race»? | A kind of blood | *The feeling of being pushed to do well | A cut that stops bleeding | The push of air in a tyre
test: Which phrase means to try hard to make someone do something? | Apply a plaster | *To pressure someone | Blood pressure | Tyre pressure
exam: Mia says pressure always means feeling stressed. What is the best reply? | *No, pressure can also mean the force of air or water on a surface | It means being happy | Only in science lessons, never with people | Yes, it always means stress
